WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.320
(Italian opera music)

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.392
- [Narrator] We all crave our
grandmother's cooking, right?

00:00:03.392 --> 00:00:05.888
Well, what if there were a
place that you could go anytime

00:00:05.888 --> 00:00:10.055
and get that home-cooked meal
from anywhere in the world?

00:00:11.481 --> 00:00:13.200
That place exists in Staten Island,

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.109
just behind that scaffolding
right over there.

00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:19.609
(dramatic operatic music)

00:00:21.453 --> 00:00:22.286
Meet Jo.

00:00:22.286 --> 00:00:24.344
He's Italian by way of Brooklyn.

00:00:24.344 --> 00:00:28.061
- My name is Jody Scaravella,
and I own Enoteca Maria.

00:00:28.061 --> 00:00:31.952
We don't employ chefs;
we employ grandmothers.

00:00:31.952 --> 00:00:33.992
- [Narrator] When Joe opened
the restaurant in 2006,

00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:35.301
he noticed something.

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:37.672
- Every culture was
coming to the restaurant

00:00:37.672 --> 00:00:40.712
to celebrate these Italian grandmothers,

00:00:40.712 --> 00:00:42.901
so I thought to myself,
“How nice would it be

00:00:42.901 --> 00:00:45.381
if we celebrated everybody's culture?”

00:00:45.381 --> 00:00:48.381
- [Narrator] And so, Nonnas
of the World was born.

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:49.712
(grandmas talking)

00:00:49.712 --> 00:00:51.630
- [Jody] There are probably somewhere

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:55.483
between 30 and 40 grandmothers
from different countries.

00:00:55.483 --> 00:00:57.220
- My name is Habiba.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:58.491
I'm from Algeria.

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:02.491
- My name is Lubof, and I
am from Moscow, Russia.

00:01:03.521 --> 00:01:04.807
- I come from Sri Lanka.

00:01:04.807 --> 00:01:05.640
- Bulgaria.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:06.473
- Dublin, Ireland.

00:01:06.473 --> 00:01:07.367
- From Armenia.

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:09.534
- Buenos Aires, Argentina.

00:01:10.487 --> 00:01:12.647
- [Narrator] Each night, there
are two grandmothers cooking.

00:01:12.647 --> 00:01:15.906
One is always Italian, the
other, from somewhere else.

00:01:15.906 --> 00:01:17.727
- [Jody] When they're
in the kitchen cooking,

00:01:17.727 --> 00:01:19.386
it's a completely different energy.

00:01:19.386 --> 00:01:22.687
It's their day to shine;
it's their day to show

00:01:22.687 --> 00:01:25.978
what their cultural cuisine is all about.

00:01:25.978 --> 00:01:27.636
You know, it sounds kind of corny,

00:01:27.636 --> 00:01:29.498
but they do cook with love.

00:01:29.498 --> 00:01:30.756
- [Narrator] And that
feeling for Jo is about more

00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:32.314
than the restaurant.

00:01:32.314 --> 00:01:36.481
- When I opened the Enoteca,
I was really grief-stricken.

00:01:37.704 --> 00:01:40.903
My mom and my sister had passed.

00:01:40.903 --> 00:01:42.222
(grandmother chatters)

00:01:42.222 --> 00:01:44.487
- It was comforting to
have these grandmothers

00:01:44.487 --> 00:01:47.629
in the kitchen cooking and, you know,
they would pinch my cheek

00:01:47.629 --> 00:01:49.160
and say, "You want to try this?"

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:53.109
And at that point, I realized
that what we were doing

00:01:53.109 --> 00:01:56.205
was much bigger than I
thought it was going to be.

00:01:56.205 --> 00:01:57.248
(glasses clinking)

00:01:57.248 --> 00:01:59.528
When you walk through the
restaurant, if you listen,

00:01:59.528 --> 00:02:01.627
every table that you pass by,

00:02:01.627 --> 00:02:03.176
they're talking about their mothers

00:02:03.176 --> 00:02:05.953
or they're talking about
their grandmothers.

00:02:05.953 --> 00:02:09.259
It kind of evokes a trip down memory lane.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:12.297
(upbeat music)

00:02:12.297 --> 00:02:14.316
(background chatter)

00:02:14.316 --> 00:02:16.316
(chime)

